# Task 156-010 — Runtime File Lock (flock spawn-time) Execution Plan

## Goal
Spawn-time concurrent-write conflict detection: before a worker container starts,
acquire a per-file spawn lock for each `scope.filesWrite` entry. If another
currently-active task already holds a spawn lock on any of those files, the
spawn aborts and surfaces a clear `SpawnBackendError`.

## Design

### New module API (src/core/file-lock.ts)
- `SpawnLockInfo` interface — { filePath, taskId, acquiredAt }.
- `SpawnLockError extends Error` — thrown when another task holds the file.
  Carries `filePath`, `conflictingTaskId` for actionable diagnostics.
- `acquireSpawnLock(projectRoot, taskId, filePath): SpawnLockInfo`
  - Atomic O_EXCL create at `.locks/<sha256(filePath).slice(0,32)>.spawnlock`.
  - Hash-based naming keeps long paths short and platform-safe.
  - Idempotent: same `taskId` re-acquiring returns the existing lock.
  - Conflict (different `taskId`): throws `SpawnLockError`.
- `releaseSpawnLock(projectRoot, taskId, filePath)` — no-op if missing;
  refuses to delete locks owned by a different task (defensive).
- `acquireSpawnLocks(projectRoot, taskId, files[]): SpawnLockInfo[]`
  - Batch helper: rolls back all previously-acquired locks if any file in the
    batch fails, so partial-lock state never leaks.
- `releaseSpawnLocks(projectRoot, taskId, files[])` — best-effort cleanup.
- `releaseAllSpawnLocks(projectRoot, taskId): number` — release every
  spawnlock owned by the task (used by monitorContainer on exit).
- `.spawnlock` extension (distinct from worker `.lock`) → existing
  `checkLocks` / `clearStaleLocks` / `clearOrphanLocks` filters
  (`endsWith('.lock')`) are unchanged. Verified — they remain limited
  to worker-time `.lock` files.

### Docker backend integration (src/orchestra/spawn-backend-docker.ts)
- Inside `spawn()`, BEFORE the docker-images precheck and prompt write:
  read the task JSON (`<tasksDir>/task-<taskId>.json`) to recover
  `scope.filesWrite`. If the file is missing or malformed, log via
  `debugLog` and skip locking (graceful degradation — never block spawn
  because of a parse failure).
- Call `acquireSpawnLocks(projectDir, taskId, filesWrite)`. On
  `SpawnLockError`: throw `SpawnBackendError` with the conflicting task id.
- On container exit (inside `monitorContainer`), call
  `releaseAllSpawnLocks(projectDir, taskId)` so future spawns can claim
  the same files.
- On `kill()`: also call `releaseAllSpawnLocks` to ensure forced shutdown
  doesn't leak locks.

## Files Touched
- src/core/file-lock.ts — additive (new exports, no breakage).
- src/orchestra/spawn-backend-docker.ts — wire acquire/release calls.
- tests/core/file-lock-runtime.test.ts — new test file.

## Test Plan (tests/core/file-lock-runtime.test.ts)
1. Single spawn lock acquired and visible on disk under `.locks/`.
2. Second `acquireSpawnLock` for same file + different taskId throws
   `SpawnLockError` (this is the canonical "2nd worker fails" scenario).
3. Idempotent re-acquire for the same taskId returns without error.
4. `releaseSpawnLock` removes the lock; subsequent acquire from a
   different taskId succeeds.
5. `acquireSpawnLocks` rolls back: if file[1] conflicts, file[0]'s lock
   is released.
6. `releaseAllSpawnLocks` removes only locks owned by the calling task.
7. Verify `.spawnlock` files don't collide with `.lock` files (existing
   `checkLocks()` ignores them).

## Verification
- `npx tsc --noEmit` — no type regressions.
- `npx vitest run tests/core/file-lock-runtime.test.ts` — new suite passes.
- `npx vitest run tests/core/file-lock.test.ts` — pre-existing suite
  still passes (no regression on the old API).

## ADR Compliance
- ADR-001/002 (TypeScript + ESM): all imports use `.js` suffix.
- ADR-008 (Brain-only import): file-lock.ts has no brain imports;
  spawn-backend-docker.ts continues to call file-lock from orchestra
  scope (already an established pattern via brain → backend → core).
- No build invocation (ANCHOR rule); no project-wide test run.
